# {{.Pocket}} — a gopernicus pocket

Scaffolded by `gopernicus new pocket`. A datastore-free, view-free pocket
module carrying one aggregate, **{{.Agg}}**, with a create/get/list/delete
use-case set. Born conforming to the pocket charter (`pockets/README.md`) — a
placeholder you rename and grow.

## Layout

```
{{.Pocket}}.go                     composition: Repositories and NewService
logic/{{.Agg}}/                     public Service, options, entity, Storer port, order allow-list
stores/storetest/                   the exported conformance suite (Run)
stores/memory/                      a usable memory Storer (same module as the core)
stores/turso/                       the Turso/libSQL store adapter (its own module)
stores/pgx/                         the PostgreSQL store adapter (its own module)
```

The public `{{.Agg}}.Service` implements the use cases and keeps its state private.
Hosts may construct it directly or use the root constructor. Add public HTTP
adapters under `inbound/http` when needed; middleware and handlers consume logic
services, and logic stays transport independent. Use `internal` only for a real
private implementation boundary. The skeleton needs no HTTP adapter or Register
method because it has no routes.

## Charter checklist trace (pockets/README.md §8)

| # | item | where |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | compiles standalone with its own go.mod | `go build ./...` |
| 2 | go.mod requires SDK/shared pockets only (FS1) | `go.mod` |
| 4 | explicit construction and public use cases | `{{.Pocket}}.go`, `logic/{{.Agg}}/service.go` |
| 5 | each store: Repositories(ctx, db) + ExportMigrations | `stores/{turso,pgx}` |
| 6 | zero-infra proof (in-memory Repositories) | `stores/memory/` |
| 10 | storetest suite + reference passes it | `stores/storetest/`, `stores/memory/conformance_test.go` |
| 11 | both {turso, pgx} stores exist and pass storetest | `stores/{turso,pgx}` |
| 13 | order allow-list + DefaultOrder + ListLimits; six-case family | `logic/{{.Agg}}/order.go`, `stores/storetest/` |
| 14 | WithIDs threaded to the entity constructor; DBGeneratedIDOnEmpty | `logic/{{.Agg}}/service.go`, `stores/storetest/` |

Items 3, 7, 8, 9, 12 hold by construction (no cross-pocket imports, no init(),
documented nil semantics). Fill route-surface docs (item 7) when you add an
inbound adapter.

## Resolving the framework

Framework dependencies are pinned to tagged releases. Run `go mod tidy` in the
core and each store module to fetch them. The store modules reach this core
through the emitted
`replace {{.ModulePath}} => ../..`; its `v0.0.0` requirement stands in until you
tag the pocket yourself.

To develop against a local gopernicus checkout instead of the tags, add a
replace in the core and in each store module (drop them before relying on tagged
resolution again):

```
go mod edit -replace github.com/gopernicus/gopernicus/sdk=/abs/path/to/gopernicus/sdk
go mod edit -replace github.com/gopernicus/gopernicus/pockets=/abs/path/to/gopernicus/pockets
go mod tidy
```

## Wiring this pocket into a host

Construct the service in your host's composition root. Mounting is optional
when an HTTP adapter exists; there is no init() registration or service locator:

```go
import (
	{{.Pocket}} "{{.ModulePath}}"
	"{{.ModulePath}}/stores/memory"          // or a store adapter's Repositories(ctx, db)
	"{{.ModulePath}}/logic/{{.Agg}}"
	"github.com/gopernicus/gopernicus/sdk/pkg/list"
)

repos := {{.Pocket}}.Repositories{ {{.ReposField}}: memory.New{{.ReposField}}() }
svc, err := {{.Pocket}}.NewService(repos)
if err != nil {
	return err
}
// Call use cases from your handlers or workers:
created, err := svc.Create(ctx, "first {{.Agg}}")
page, err := svc.List(ctx, {{.Agg}}.ListFilter{}, list.Request{})
```

For a persistent host, replace the memory adapter with a driver adapter:

```go
repos, err := turso.Repositories(ctx, db)   // or pgx.Repositories(ctx, db)
```

## Migrations (scaffold-and-own)

Store adapters expose canonical SQL and `ExportMigrations`, but the **host** owns
its migration tree. Scaffold this pocket's SQL into your host's ledger and apply
it pre-boot — the framework never migrates at startup:

```go
turso.ExportMigrations("workshop/migrations/primary")   // or pgx.ExportMigrations(...)
```

Both dialect trees carry an identical migration version set (`0001_{{.Agg}}.sql`);
the host resolves any filename conflicts in its own directory.

## Rename it

The `{{.Agg}}` aggregate and its single `Name` field are placeholders. To model a
real aggregate: edit `logic/{{.Agg}}/{{.Agg}}.go` (entity + fields), extend the
`Storer` port and the service, mirror the columns in both stores' row structs and
`migrations/0001_{{.Agg}}.sql`, and add cases to `storetest`. The SQL table is
named `{{.Agg}}` — rename it if it collides with a reserved word.
